Said: "the gate at the allotment sticks unless you lift it." -> {"answer": "yes"}
Said: "one day I'll learn to sail." -> {"answer": "no"}
Said: "my brother takes the dog on Thursdays." -> {"answer": "yes"}
Said: "we could put bunk beds in the back room eventually." -> {"answer": "no"}

Said: "actually we settled on Dr Croft for the dog." ->
Reply with ONLY the JSON object: {"answer": "yes"} or {"answer": "no"}

An assistant searched a person's own files to answer their question, then wrote a reply.
Question: does the reply give what the person asked for?
Answer {"answer": "yes"} when the reply states the thing asked for: a value, a name, a date, a place, or what a document says about it.
Answer {"answer": "no"} when the reply only reports that it was not found, is not there, could not be checked, or asks where to look, and does not give the thing asked for.

Asked: "when does my car insurance run out?" Reply: "Your policy document says cover ends on 14 March." -> {"answer": "yes"}
Asked: "what is my boiler's model number?" Reply: "I went through your documents and none of them gives a boiler model. Want me to try another folder?" -> {"answer": "no"}
Asked: "who is my landlord?" Reply: "The tenancy agreement names Harlow Lettings as the landlord." -> {"answer": "yes"}
Asked: "how many guests are coming on Saturday?" Reply: "Nothing I opened says how many guests are coming." -> {"answer": "no"}
Asked: "what time is the school play?" Reply: "The newsletter lists the school play at 6:30 pm on Thursday." -> {"answer": "yes"}
Asked: "what is my bike frame size?" Reply: "That detail does not appear anywhere in what I can read on this computer." -> {"answer": "no"}

Asked: "who is my chimney sweep?" Reply: "Your chimney sweep is booked through the letting agent (see Documents/chimney-sweep.txt). The file doesn't give a name — it says the sweep will ring the day before. Want me to look for the letting agent's details?" ->
Reply with ONLY the JSON object: {"answer": "yes"} or {"answer": "no"}

A person wrote one sentence to their assistant. It is shown in two parts: the words in front, then the rest.
Question: is the person asking the ASSISTANT to forget or erase something the assistant knows, was told or saved about them (to remove it from the assistant's memory)?
Answer {"answer": "yes"} or {"answer": "no"}. A sentence about forgetting in general, about the person's own forgetting, about dropping a plan or an idea, about a device or a file, or one that asks how to forget something, is no.

Front: "you can" Rest: "forget my gym locker number" -> {"answer": "yes"}
Front: "you can" Rest: "forget about going to the beach, it is raining" -> {"answer": "no"}
